= 2. Systems of Linear Equations.
<systems-of-linear-equations.>
A system of equations is a collection of equations that (usually) share
the same variables. The following is a system: $ 2 x + 3 y & = 5\
3 x - 4 y = - 1 . $ A solution to a system of equations is a value given
to each variable that makes each equation in the system true. The system
above has solution $x = 1$ and $y = 1$. Another way to write this is
$(1 , 1)$, since the two lines intersect at that point. \
Graph the two lines and verify that the solution given is where the two
lines intersect. \
\
Are there any systems that have no solutions? \
Are there any systems that have more than one solution? \
There are many ways of solving a system of equations. We will discuss
three ways in this section.

+ Graphing: if you graph all equations on the same coordinate plane, you
  can visually identify where all of the equations meet. A solution is
  any point where each equation passes through.

+ Substitution: if you can isolate one variable in one of the equations,
  you can substitute that variable into the other equation. From this
  point you have one equation with one variable, and you can use any
  technique to solve for that variable. Once you know the value of one
  variable, you can substitute that back in to either of the original
  equations and solve for the other variable.

+ Elimination: if you add or subtract one equation from the other, you
  can often eliminate a variable. Once you eliminate one variable, you
  can now solve for the other variable. Just like with substitution,
  once you know the value of one variable, you can use it to find the
  other.

Use substitution to solve the above system. \
Use elimination to solve the above system. \
If each equation in our system of equations is a linear equation, we
call that system a linear system, or a system of linear equations. In
any linear system, there are three possible outcomes for a solution:

+ Exactly one solution: each line passes through the same point.

+ No solution: lines are parallel and share no common points.

+ Infinitely many solutions: lines overlap.

If a system has at least one solution we call that system "consistent,"
and if it has no solutions we call that system "inconsistent."

